# User defined data type

In a class, there are students, and all students have some common
 attributes like roll number, percentage, name, and height.

Every student is an object of the class, and each object has these
common properties.

The blueprint that defines these common properties and behavior
is known as a class.



For example, in a class, there are 50 students, and we need to store
their data.

We can create a String array to store their names, an int array
for roll numbers, and a double array for percentages. However,
these separate arrays are not linked together, making it difficult
to manage student data as a single unit.

This is why we use Classes in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).

A class allows us to group related data (name, roll number, percentage)
into a single entity, making it easier to manage and work with student
records.



##  Why OOP is Better Than Normal (Procedural) Approach
Without OOP: Data is stored in separate arrays , making it hard to
             keep track.

With OOP: All student details (name , rno , percent ) are stored
          inside a single object.

Example (Without OOP - Separate Arrays)

String[] names = {"Deepak", "Rahul", "Anjali"};
int[] rnos = {26, 14, 32};
double[] percents = {56.68, 78.9, 85.5};

Problem: If you delete one student's name, you must also delete their
         roll number and percentage manually from other arrays.

Example (With OOP - Objects)
Student s1 = new Student("Deepak", 26, 56.68);
Student s2 = new Student("Rahul", 14, 78.9);
Student s3 = new Student("Anjali", 32, 85.5);

Solution: Data stays together, making it easier to manage.

Code Reusability 🔄
Without OOP: If we want to print student details, we write the same
              logic multiple times.
With OOP: We can use methods inside the class, avoiding duplicate code.

Example (Without OOP - Repeating Code)
System.out.println(names[0] + " " + rnos[0] + " " + percents[0]);
System.out.println(names[1] + " " + rnos[1] + " " + percents[1]);
System.out.println(names[2] + " " + rnos[2] + " " + percents[2]);

Example (With OOP - Using a Method)
class Student {
    String name;
    int rno;
    double percent;

  void printDetails() {
    System.out.println("Name: " + name + ", Roll No: " + rno + ", Percentage: " + percent);
  }
}
✅ Solution: One method works for all students, reducing code duplication.

3️⃣ Scalability & Maintainability 🏗️
Without OOP: Adding a new student field (e.g., address) means adding
             a new array.
With OOP: We just add a new variable inside the Student class.

Example (Adding Address - Without OOP)

String[] addresses = {"Delhi", "Mumbai", "Pune"}; // More arrays 😞


Example (Adding Address - With OOP)

class Student {
    String name, address;
    int rno;
    double percent;
}
✅ Solution: Easier to extend without breaking existing code.

4️⃣ Encapsulation (Data Security & Control) 🔒
Without OOP: Anyone can modify the student data directly.
With OOP: We can control access using private variables and
getters/setters.

✅ Final Verdict
Feature	             Without OOP (Procedural)	With OOP (Objects)
Data Organization	 Uses multiple arrays 📂	Uses a single class 🎯
Code Reusability	 Repeated code ❌	        Uses methods 🔄
Scalability	         Hard to extend 😞	        Easy to extend 🚀
Data Security	     No control ❌	            Encapsulation 🔒
Real-World Modeling	 Not intuitive 📉	        Realistic 🎯

✅ Use OOP when dealing with structured data like students,
    employees, products, etc.
✅ Use procedural programming for small, simple scripts.


object - creation

className  objectName  =  new className();
* Objects are real life entities also called instance of class.
* classes are blueprints

A class defines the structure and behavior but does not exist physically.
It is like a blueprint for a house—describing how the house should be
built (rooms, doors, windows) but not an actual house itself.

# dynamically object creation

syntax:
Test obj = (Test) Class.forName("com.p1.Test").newInstance();

🔹 Class.forName("com.p1.Test")
This loads the class Test from package com.p1.
The class must be present in your project.
It returns a Class object representing Test.

🔹 .newInstance()
This creates a new object of Test using the default constructor (without parameters).
It is similar to new Test(), but done dynamically.

🔹 (Test)
newInstance() returns an Object type.
So, we typecast it to Test to use it properly.

Scenario:
Imagine you're creating a game where users can choose different characters:
"Warrior"
"Mage"
"Archer"

Warrior obj = new Warrior(); // Fixed class

We can dynamically create an object based on user input:
String className = "Warrior"; // This can change based on user choice
Character obj = (Character) Class.forName(className).newInstance();

Conclusion
Dynamically = "At runtime, not hardcoded"
Instead of new Test(), we load the class and create an object during execution.
Useful for flexible and modular programs.


import java.util.Scanner;

class Warrior {
    void attack() {
        System.out.println("Warrior swings a sword!");
    }
}

class Mage {
    void attack() {
        System.out.println("Mage casts a fireball!");
    }
}

class Archer {
    void attack() {
        System.out.println("Archer shoots an arrow!");
    }
}

public class SimpleDynamicClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter character (Warrior, Mage, Archer):");
        String className = scanner.nextLine(); // Get user input

        try {
            // Load class dynamically
            Object obj = Class.forName(className).newInstance();

            // Call attack() method
            obj.getClass().getMethod("attack").invoke(obj);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error: Invalid class name");
        }

        scanner.close();
    }
}

 How This Works
User enters a class name (Warrior, Mage, or Archer).
Program loads the class dynamically at runtime.
It creates an object of the selected class.
Calls the attack() method using reflection.

Enter character (Warrior, Mage, Archer):
Warrior
Warrior swings a sword!
....


