## Key Points About Instance Variables:-

Declared inside a class but outside any method, constructor, or block.
Each object of the class gets its own copy of instance variables.
They belong to an instance (object), not the class itself.
Access modifiers (private, public, etc.) control visibility.

class Student {
    private int rno;  // Instance variable (Each object will have its own rno)

    public void setRno(int rno) {
        this.rno = rno;  // 'this.rno' refers to the instance variable
    }

    public int getRno() {
        return rno;  // Returns the instance variable
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.setRno(101);

        Student s2 = new Student();
        s2.setRno(102);

        System.out.println(s1.getRno()); // Output: 101 ✅
        System.out.println(s2.getRno()); // Output: 102 ✅ (Different object, different value)
    }
}



##  Class in a Different File but Same Package

If two classes are in the same package but in different files,
you can access the properties and methods without an import statement
(as long as they are not private).
You can directly create an object of one class in another class.

.The behavior of class in diff Package
Must use import packageName.ClassName; before using the class.


