## Access Modifiers
1) Public - all package
2) Private - same class
3) Default - same package (also known as package-private)
4) protected - same class , subclass, even outside the package

Correct Accessibility Order (from most to least accessible):
public > protected > default (package-private) > private


## Getters and Setters
This are basically function of a class

Usually we can't change the private attributes and we can't access ,,,

In Java, getters and setters are methods used to access and modify
private attributes of a class while maintaining encapsulation.

Getters (Accessors) → Used to retrieve (read) the value of a private attribute.
Setters (Mutators) → Used to update (write) the value of a private
                     attribute, usually with validation.

public class Vehicle {
  private String color;

   public String getColor() {
    return color;
   }

  public void setColor(String c) {
   this.color = c;
  }
}

The getter method returns the value of the attribute. The setter method takes a
parameter and assigns it to the attribute.


What is a keyword ?
Keyword is a word which is reserved by the programming language. That is,
a keyword has a special meaning  so it can’t be used as the name of some
variable/class/function. A keyword has a special meaning which
defines specific parameters of the program.


## 'this'  Keyword

 private int rno;

 public void setRno(int rno) {  // Setter
    rno = rno;  // ❌ WRONG: Local variable 'rno' is assigned to itself
 }

 Here, rno = rno; means the method parameter (rno) is assigned
 to itself instead of modifying the instance variable.
 The instance variable remains unchanged, and it does not update
 the object's data.

What Actually Happens Here?
The instance variable rno starts with 0 (default value).
Inside setRno(int rno), Java sees rno = rno;
Left rno → Java thinks this is the method parameter.
Right rno → Java also thinks this is the method parameter.
So, you're just assigning the parameter to itself! (No effect on the instance variable)
The instance variable remains unchanged (0).
getRno() returns 0, so the output is 0.


   public void setRno(int rno){  //setter
         this.rno = rno;
     }


this.rno = rno; → this.rno refers to instance variable, and rno refers
to method parameter.
Without this, Java assumes you're referring to the method parameter only,
so it doesn't modify the actual object.



## What is Constructor

 A constructor has the same name as the class and does not have a
 return type (not even void).

 To provide a single entry point for object setup.

 Key Points About Constructors:
 The constructor name must match the class name.
 It does not have an explicit return type.
 It is automatically called when an object is created.
 It is used to initialize object properties


## What is final keyword
When a variable is declared final, its value cannot be changed after initialization.
If it is a primitive type, the value is constant.
If it is a reference type (object), the reference cannot be changed,
but the object's internal data can still be modified.


## What is static Keyword

Class Student{
  String name;
  int rno;
  double percent;
  int numberOfStudents;
}

 public  Student(String name, int rno , double percent){
        this.name = name;
        this.rno = rno;
        this.percent = percent;
        numberOfStudents++;
    }

 Student s1= new Student("Rahul" , 67 , 98.3);
 System.out.println(s1.numberOfStudents);

 Student s2= new Student("R" , 7 , 8.3);
 System.out.println(s2.numberOfStudents);

 output will be :
 1
 1

 so actually what happen here that is when initialization of object
 so memory will make space for each attributes ,for every object it
 will make one extra occupies space that is for numberOfStudents ,
 increment it will become 0 to 1 so that's  why we got 1 value for each
 object also  numberOfStudents object depended.

 To avoid this extra space we need to make one space and also
 numberOfStudents class depended and use everywhere.
 so make this space when creation of class so we use static keyword

 Class Student{
   String name;
   int rno;
   double percent;
   static int numberOfStudents;
 }

 write all code then output will be:
 1
 2

  Student s1= new Student("Rahul" , 67 , 98.3);
  Student s2= new Student("R" , 7 , 8.3);
  System.out.println(s2.numberOfStudents);
  System.out.println(s1.numberOfStudents);

  the output will be
  2
  2



